<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//W3C//DTD HTML 4.0 Transitional//EN">
<html>
<head>
	<title>Installing Xymon</title>
</head>

<body>
<h1>Installing Xymon</h1>

<p><b>From a package or from source?</b> If your operating system or its ports
collection packages Xymon, or you use Homebrew on a Mac, install that: see
<a href="#packages">Installing from a package</a>. Otherwise, follow the six steps
below. Problems and explanations are at the end.</p>

<h2>1. Install the prerequisites</h2>
<p>As root, run the command for your system. A C compiler and OpenSSL come with the
base system on the BSDs.</p>
<table border="1" cellpadding="4" cellspacing="0">
<tr><th>System</th><th>Command</th><th>Note</th></tr>
<tr><td>Debian, Ubuntu</td><td><tt>apt-get install build-essential fping libc-ares-dev libldap-dev libpcre2-dev librrd-dev libssl-dev libtirpc-dev</tt></td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Red Hat Enterprise Linux 8 to 10</td><td><tt>subscription-manager repos --enable codeready-builder-for-rhel-<i>N</i>-$(arch)-rpms<br>dnf install https://dl.fedoraproject.org/pub/epel/epel-release-latest-<i>N</i>.noarch.rpm<br>dnf install gcc make fping c-ares-devel openldap-devel pcre2-devel rrdtool-devel openssl-devel libtirpc-devel</tt></td><td><i>N</i> is the version: 8, 9 or 10.</td></tr>
<tr><td>AlmaLinux, Rocky Linux, Oracle Linux 8 to 10</td><td><tt>dnf install dnf-plugins-core<br>dnf config-manager --set-enabled crb<br>dnf install epel-release<br>dnf install gcc make fping c-ares-devel openldap-devel pcre2-devel rrdtool-devel openssl-devel libtirpc-devel</tt></td><td>On version 8 the repository is "powertools", not "crb". On Oracle Linux it is "ol<i>N</i>_codeready_builder", and EPEL is "oracle-epel-release-el<i>N</i>".</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fedora</td><td><tt>dnf install gcc make fping c-ares-devel openldap-devel pcre2-devel rrdtool-devel openssl-devel libtirpc-devel</tt></td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>openSUSE</td><td><tt>zypper install gcc make gawk fping c-ares-devel openldap2-devel pcre2-devel rrdtool-devel libopenssl-devel libtirpc-devel</tt></td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Alpine Linux</td><td><tt>apk add build-base fping c-ares-dev openldap-dev pcre2-dev rrdtool-dev openssl-dev libtirpc-dev</tt></td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Arch Linux</td><td><tt>pacman -S base-devel fping c-ares openldap pcre2 rrdtool openssl libtirpc</tt></td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>FreeBSD</td><td><tt>pkg install gmake fping c-ares openldap26-client pcre2 rrdtool</tt></td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>OpenBSD</td><td><tt>pkg_add gmake fping libcares openldap-client--gssapi pcre2 rrdtool</tt></td><td>openldap-client also comes in a plain flavor; the tests build with <i>gssapi</i>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>NetBSD</td><td><tt>pkgin install gmake fping libcares openldap-client pcre2 rrdtool</tt></td><td>Without X11 installed, see <a href="#nographs">no graphs</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>macOS, MacPorts</td><td><tt>port install gmake fping pcre2 rrdtool c-ares openssl openldap</tt></td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>macOS, Homebrew</td><td><tt>brew install pcre2 rrdtool fping openssl@3 openldap</tt></td><td>Or install Xymon itself from the tap: see <a href="#packages">packages</a>.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>2. Create the "xymon" user</h2>
<p>Xymon runs as a dedicated user with a home directory, e.g. <tt>useradd -m xymon</tt>.
configure offers that home directory as the place to install Xymon.</p>

<h2>3. Build and install</h2>
<p>In the unpacked Xymon source, as root, accepting configure's default answers:</p>
<pre><tt>./configure --server
make
make install
</tt></pre>
<p>On the BSDs and macOS, use GNU make: <tt>MAKE=gmake ./configure --server</tt>,
<tt>gmake</tt>, <tt>gmake install</tt>.</p>

<h2>4. Configure the web server</h2>
<p>Add <em>~xymon/server/etc/xymon-apache.conf</em> to your Apache configuration
and restart Apache. <a name="htpasswd"></a>If configure put the administration pages in a directory of their
own (it asks), protect them with a password:</p>
<pre><tt>htpasswd -c @INSTALLETCDIR@/xymonpasswd admin
</tt></pre>

<h2>5. Start Xymon</h2>
<pre><tt>su - xymon -c "~xymon/server/xymon.sh start"
</tt></pre>
<p>(<tt>~xymon/server</tt> with configure's default answers.)</p>

<h2>6. Check</h2>
<p>After a couple of minutes, open <tt>http://your.server/xymon/</tt>: the Xymon server
shows its own statuses. To start Xymon at boot, see
<a href="#boot">Starting Xymon at boot</a>.</p>

<h2>Next steps</h2>

<p>Congratulations, you now have a running Xymon server!</p>
<p>The next step is to configure it to monitor your servers and applications, and to set up
the alerts to send you e-mail, call a pager, or send an SMS in case of trouble. For that, see
the <a href="xymon-config.html">Xymon configuration guide</a>.</p>


<h2><a name="packages">Installing from a package</a></h2>
<p>A package installs the prerequisites itself, and usually starts Xymon at boot.
Distribution packages follow Xymon's releases; building from source, as above,
gives the current code.</p>
<table border="1" cellpadding="4" cellspacing="0">
<tr><th>System</th><th>Package</th></tr>
<tr><td>Debian, Ubuntu</td><td><tt>apt-get install xymon</tt> (server) or <tt>xymon-client</tt></td></tr>
<tr><td>FreeBSD</td><td>ports <tt>net-mgmt/xymon-server</tt> and <tt>net-mgmt/xymon-client</tt></td></tr>
<tr><td>NetBSD</td><td>pkgsrc <tt>net/xymon</tt></td></tr>
<tr><td>Red Hat Enterprise Linux and its rebuilds 8 to 10, x86_64</td><td><a href="https://github.com/xymon-monitoring/xymon-rpm">xymon-rpm</a>: highly experimental, not for production yet; see its README</td></tr>
<tr><td>macOS</td><td>the Homebrew tap, below</td></tr>
</table>
<h4><a name="commonmacoshomebrew">macOS (Apple Silicon and Intel) using Homebrew</a></h4>
<p>The easiest way to install Xymon on a modern Mac is through the official
<a href="https://github.com/xymon-monitoring/homebrew-xymon">Homebrew tap</a>,
which provides a full server formula and a lighter client-only formula:</p>
<pre><tt>
brew tap xymon-monitoring/xymon
brew install --HEAD xymon-monitoring/xymon/xymon-server     # full server
brew install --HEAD xymon-monitoring/xymon/xymon-client     # client only
</tt></pre>
<p>and run it under launchd with:</p>
<pre><tt>
brew services start xymon-server      # or: xymon-client
</tt></pre>
<p>The formulas install everything under the Homebrew prefix (the server tree
ends up in <tt>$(brew --prefix)/opt/xymon-server/server</tt>) and provide a
launchd service. They do <i>not</i> create a dedicated "xymon" user or
configure a webserver - edit <tt>etc/hosts.cfg</tt> (server) or the XYMSRV
setting in <tt>etc/xymonclient.cfg</tt> (client), and set up the web frontend
as described in the "Configuring your webserver" section above. As no Xymon
release tarballs are published yet, the formulas are <tt>--HEAD</tt>-only and
build the latest Git revision; see the
<a href="https://github.com/xymon-monitoring/homebrew-xymon">tap README</a>
for how to upgrade a HEAD install. The SYSV IPC requirements described in the
prerequisites apply on macOS too, where the stock limits are too low for xymond;
the xymon-server formula's caveats, shown by <tt>brew info xymon-server</tt>, give
the settings to raise. They allow 64 segments, 64 MB per segment and 128 MB in all,
enough for a MAXMSG_STATUS up to about 40 MB.</p>

<p>To build from source instead, install the dependencies with Homebrew:</p>
<pre><tt>
brew install pcre2 rrdtool fping openssl@3 openldap
</tt></pre>
<p>then run <tt>./configure --server</tt>, <tt>make</tt> and <tt>make install</tt>
as described above. The configure script automatically detects libraries
installed under the Homebrew prefix (/opt/homebrew on Apple Silicon, /usr/local
on Intel Macs), including the keg-only openssl@3 and openldap packages which
Homebrew does not symlink into the main prefix; MacPorts (/opt/local) is
detected as well.</p>

<p><b>Testing a development branch or pull request:</b> the formulas always
build the "main" branch. To build a feature branch instead (e.g. to test a
pending pull request on a Mac), point the <tt>head</tt> stanza of your local
tap checkout at that branch and rebuild:</p>
<pre><tt>
brew edit xymon-monitoring/xymon/xymon-server
   # change:  head "https://github.com/xymon-monitoring/xymon.git", branch: "main"
   # to:      head "https://github.com/&lt;fork&gt;/xymon.git", branch: "&lt;feature-branch&gt;"
brew reinstall --HEAD xymon-monitoring/xymon/xymon-server
</tt></pre>
<p>When done, restore the tap to its pristine state and rebuild from main:</p>
<pre><tt>
git -C "$(brew --repository xymon-monitoring/xymon)" checkout -- .
brew reinstall --HEAD xymon-monitoring/xymon/xymon-server
</tt></pre>
<p>Two things to be aware of: the edited tap is a dirty git checkout, so
<tt>brew update</tt> will warn about it until you revert the change; and a
HEAD reinstall replaces the entire keg <i>including its etc/ directory</i>,
so back up your edited configuration files (hosts.cfg, xymonserver.cfg,
xymonclient.cfg) before rebuilding.</p>


<h2>If something goes wrong</h2>

<h4>xymond does not start</h4>

<p>Xymon uses 9 shared memory segments, ranging in size from 32 KB to 512 KB (2464 KB
total) in the default configuration; and 9 sets of 3 semaphores. Experience shows that
some systems need tuning to provide the necessary IPC resources that Xymon uses.
Specifically, when installing on Solaris you must increase the "shmseg" kernel parameter
from the default 6 to at least 9. Since other programs on your system may also use shared
memory, a higher value may be required.</p>
<p>The total grows with the MAXMSG_* settings in xymonserver.cfg, which size the
segments: MAXMSG_STATUS sizes three of them. When the system refuses one, xymond
does not start, and logs "Could not get shm of size ..." followed by "Cannot setup
... channel". The limits on their size are "kernel.shmmax" (one segment) and
"kernel.shmall" (all of them) on Linux, the same names under "kern.sysv." on macOS,
and "project.max-shm-memory" on Solaris 10 and later. A stock macOS allows 8
segments per process, too few even for the default configuration, and 4 MB in all
(see <a href="#commonmacoshomebrew">macOS using Homebrew</a> below).  See <a href="http://www.xymon.com/archive/2005/08/msg00183.html">http://www.xymon.com/archive/2005/08/msg00183.html</a> for more information about these issues.</p>
<p>What xymond needs, in KB: the largest segment is the largest MAXMSG_* setting,
and the total is</p>
<pre><tt>
3 x MAXMSG_STATUS + 2 x MAXMSG_CLIENT + MAXMSG_DATA + MAXMSG_NOTES + MAXMSG_USER + MAXMSG_ENADIS
</tt></pre>
<p>as long as MAXMSG_STACHG and MAXMSG_PAGE are left to follow MAXMSG_STATUS, and
MAXMSG_CLICHG to follow MAXMSG_CLIENT, which is their default. For example,
MAXMSG_STATUS="1024" with the other settings at their defaults needs a 1024 KB
segment and 3 x 1024 + 2 x 512 + 256 + 256 + 128 + 32 = 4768 KB in all: shmmax
of at least 1048576 bytes, and shmall of at least 1192 pages of 4 KB. That is more
than the 4 MB a stock macOS allows.</p>
<p>The defaults of a recent Linux (kernel 3.16 and later) and of Solaris 10 and later
are usually far above this, so the limits mostly need raising on macOS and on older
systems.</p>


<h4>The build fails</h4>
<p>Note: Although many systems have these libraries pre-installed, they often
include only the run-time libraries and not the files that are needed to compile
and build programs such as Xymon. So if you think you have all of these libraries
installed but Xymon will not build, do check that you have the development
files installed as well. Often these are in packages called "something<em>-dev</em>".</p>

<p><b>HP-UX users</b> should note that the HP-supplied C compiler is known to mis-compile the
lib/environ.c file, and produces an output file lib/environ.o of length 0 bytes.
HP-UX users on the Xymon mailing list agree that the default C compiler shipped with
HP-UX should not be used to compile Xymon - it is only for re-building the HP-UX kernel.
The GNU C compiler works fine on HP-UX. More details in 
<a href="http://www.xymon.com/archive/2005/06/msg00079.html">this e-mail</a> from
the Xymon mailing list.</p>


<h4><a name="nographs">No graphs</a></h4>
<p><em>NetBSD:</em> the pre-built rrdtool package links against the base X11
	sets in <tt>/usr/X11R7</tt> (e.g. <tt>libfontconfig.so.2</tt>). These are
	not present on systems installed without X11, and cannot be added with
	pkg_add. Install the xbase set - no reinstall needed (adjust release and
	architecture):<br>
	<tt>ftp https://cdn.netbsd.org/pub/NetBSD/NetBSD-10.1/amd64/binary/sets/xbase.tar.xz</tt><br>
	<tt>tar -xJpf xbase.tar.xz -C /</tt><br>
	Without it, the RRDtool link test fails and Xymon builds with trend-graph
	support disabled.</p>

<h2>Reference</h2>

<h3>What the prerequisites are for</h3>
<ul>
	<li>PCRE2 - Perl Compatible Regular Expression library, version 2 - is a
	library for matching text-strings. It is available from
	<a href="https://www.pcre.org/">https://www.pcre.org/</a>. Xymon needs
	PCRE2; the older PCRE library (version 8.x) is no longer used. Xymon
	4.3.30 is the last release that builds with it.</li>

	<li>RRDtool is a library for handling the Round-Robin Databases used
	to hold the historical data Xymon gathers. It is available from 
	<a href="http://www.mrtg.org/rrdtool/">http://www.mrtg.org/rrdtool/</a>.
	No minimum version is set; the versions current Linux and *BSD systems
	ship, which have it pre-packaged, are the ones tested.<br>
	Note that RRDtool requires various graphics-libraries. RRDtool 1.2.x uses
	libpng, newer versions rely on the Cairo graphics library.</li>

	<li>c-ares is an asynchronous DNS resolver library, required to build the
	"xymonnet" network test tool. Xymon no longer ships a bundled copy, so the
	c-ares development files must be installed (packages are commonly named
	libc-ares-dev, c-ares-devel or c-ares). It is available from
	<a href="https://c-ares.org/">https://c-ares.org/</a></li>

	<li>fping is used for the network ping tests. Xymon's own xymonping does
	not work at the moment, so fping is required. Every system below has it as
	a package named "fping".</li>

	<li>OpenSSL is a library for communicating with network services, that
	use SSL encryption - e.g. secure websites. Although this library is
	not absolutely required for Xymon, I strongly recommend that you install
	it because sooner or later you will probably need it anyway. It is 
	available from <a href="http://www.openssl.org/">http://www.openssl.org/</a>.</li>

	<li>OpenLDAP is used to query LDAP directory servers. If you would like
	to test that your directory server is up and running, you will need this
	library. It is available from <a href="http://www.openldap.org/">http://www.openldap.org/</a></li>
</ul>


<h3>xymon.sh</h3>
<p>Xymon runs as the "xymon" user, and <tt>xymon.sh</tt> refuses to start it as
any other. Log in as that user and run<br>
<tt><i>XYMONHOME</i>/xymon.sh start</tt><br>
where <i>XYMONHOME</i> is the "server" directory of the installation - with the
default answers to configure, <tt>~xymon/server</tt>. The same script also takes
<tt>stop</tt>, <tt>restart</tt> and <tt>status</tt>; <tt>reload</tt> sends xymond a
HUP signal so it re-reads hosts.cfg, and <tt>rotate</tt> sends a HUP to
every Xymon program that keeps a pid file, so they reopen their logfiles after
a log rotation.</p>


<h3><a name="boot">Starting Xymon at boot</a></h3>

<p>Building from source installs nothing that starts Xymon when the system boots;
a Xymon package from your operating system does that for you. Otherwise, have
your system's startup run, as root, something like<br>
<tt>su - xymon -c "<i>XYMONHOME</i>/xymon.sh start"</tt><br>
and the matching <tt>stop</tt> at shutdown - through an init script, an rc.d
script, a launchd job or <tt>/etc/rc.local</tt>, whichever your system uses.
Since xymon.sh only accepts the xymon user, a script running as root must switch
to it, as above.</p>


<h3>What runs</h3>

<p>xymon.sh starts <tt>xymonlaunch</tt>, which starts and watches every other Xymon
server program. Which programs those are is set in <tt>etc/tasks.cfg</tt>; with the
default file, these run all the time:</p>
<ul>
	<li><tt>xymond</tt> is the network daemon that receives status updates from
	the clients and the network test tool. It also provides the current status of
	all your systems to the tool that generates the webpages.</li>
	<li><tt>xymond_channel</tt> passes messages from xymond to each of the helper
	programs below; there is one per helper.</li>
	<li><tt>xymond_history</tt> records the history of status changes for each
	item you monitor: when it was red, when it was green, and what the error
	reported at 2:51 AM last Friday looked like.</li>
	<li><tt>xymond_alert</tt> sends out alerts when your servers begin to report
	a critical status.</li>
	<li><tt>xymond_client</tt> analyses the data the Xymon clients send, and turns
	it into statuses such as cpu, disk and memory.</li>
	<li><tt>xymond_rrd</tt> updates the RRD files with the numeric data from the
	status reports, to track e.g. how the disk utilization of a server changes
	over time. There are two of these, because the data can arrive in two
	different ways.</li>
	<li><tt>xymond_hostdata</tt> saves the latest data a client sent whenever one of
	that host's statuses goes red, yellow or purple, so you can see what it
	reported around the time a problem occurred.</li>
</ul>
<p>Others run at intervals: <tt>xymongen</tt> updates the webpages, <tt>xymonnet</tt>
runs the network tests (and <tt>xymonnet-again.sh</tt> repeats the failing ones on a
faster cycle),
<tt>combostatus</tt> computes combined statuses, and the Xymon server's own client
reports on the server itself. More programs are listed in tasks.cfg but disabled,
for setups that need them - storing status files, proxying, fetching from clients
that cannot connect, distributing to other servers.</p>

<h3>The server's statuses</h3>
<p>Each of the little faces indicate an item that is being monitored for this host. Here
you see the default set of items that the Xymon installation sets up for a Xymon server:</p>
<ul>
	<li><em>bbd</em> is the availability of the Xymon network daemon.</li>
	<li><em>conn</em> is a simple &quot;ping&quot; test of the host.</li>
	<li><em>http</em> is the status of the HTTP-server running on the Xymon server.</li>
	<li><em>info</em> contains information about how the host is configured in Xymon,
	such as what IP-address it has, what network tests are being run against this
	host etc.</li>
	<li><em>trends</em> is a collection of the various RRD graphs available for this host.</li>
	<li><em>xymond</em> is the status of the Xymon daemon, with statistics about
	how many monitored items are being tracked.</li>
	<li><em>xymongen</em> is the status of the xymongen tool, which updates the webpages.</li>
	<li><em>xymonnet</em> is the status of the xymonnet network tester that performs
	all of the network tests you configure in Xymon.</li>
</ul>
<p>The server's own client adds its columns too: cpu, disk, memory and others.</p>
<p>You can click on each of the green icons to see a more detailed status.</p>


<h3>Upgrading</h3>
<p>It is safe to run <tt>make install</tt> when upgrading a Xymon server. It installs the 
programs, adds new template-files that were not present in your previous version,
and updates your configuration files with any new sections that have been added.
Any changes you have made yourself are preserved.</p>


<h3>configure</h3>
<p>You can <a href="configure.txt">see what configure looks like</a>, and how the
Apache configuration it installs looks <a href="xymon-apacheconf.txt">here</a>.</p>
</body>
</html>
